      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2017 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To evaluate clinical outcome of patients with Ellis-van Creveld syndrome (EVC) in whom congenital heart disease (CHD) repair was delayed intentionally to reduce the risk of postoperative respiratory morbidity and mortality."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
            "content": "This retrospective review of 51 EVC c.1886+5G>T homozygotes born between 2005 and 2014 focused on 18 subjects who underwent surgery for CHD, subdivided into early (mean, 1.3 months) vs delayed (mean, 50.1 months) repair."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Growth trajectories differed between control subjects and patients with EVC, and CHD was associated with slower weight gain. Relative to controls, infants with EVC had a 40%-75% higher respiratory rates (independent of CHD) accompanied by signs of compensated respiratory acidosis. Blood gases and respiratory rates approached normal values by age 4 years. Hemodynamically significant CHD was present in 23 children, 18 (78%) of whom underwent surgical repair. Surgery was performed at 1.3\u2009±\u20091.3 months for children born between 2005 and 2009 (n\u2009=\u20099) and 50.1\u2009±\u200940.2 months (P\u2009=\u2009.009) for children born between 2010 and 2014 (n\u2009=\u20099). The latter had shorter postoperative mechanical ventilation (1.1\u2009±\u20092.4 days vs 49.6\u2009±\u200957.1 days; P\u2009=\u2009.075), shorter intensive care duration of stay (16\u2009±\u200924 days vs 48.6\u2009±\u200944.2 days; P\u2009=\u2009.155), and no postoperative tracheostomies (vs 60%; P\u2009=\u2009.028) or deaths (vs 44%; P\u2009=\u2009.082)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Among children with EVC and possibly other short-rib thoracic dysplasias, delayed surgical repair of CHD reduces postoperative morbidity and improves survival. Respiratory rate serves as a simple indicator for optimal timing of surgical repair."
          }
        ]
      },
